A33 — blank the output on the session-end path

The reaper (A27/A28) ends the session when the game's process is gone, but wine's
virtual desktop window outlives the game: by then it is the compositor's only
remaining client, so the last commit -- and what the display is left holding --
is wine's desktop background. The compositor already waits on the reaper's exit
to end the session; hold the output black there, and paint one black frame
before the loop stops.

The same desktop background is what the panel is left holding at the other end:
from startup until the guest's first frame arrives, wine's desktop is the only
content the compositor has. So the blank is raised earlier too -- whenever the
game's window is gone while the session is still alive -- and a window that has
never had an override surface is never blanked.

What tells the desktop from the game's content is time, not surface. The first
round of this patch judged it by the surface of the window's newest done commit
(a commit from the window's own surface was taken to be the desktop), which
holds for a Vulkan guest but not for one that renders through GL: the xlib
libGL hands its frames to Xwayland with XShmPutImage, so they arrive on the
window's own surface, and such a guest may still have bound an override surface
for a Vulkan swapchain it never presents on (Tomb Raider I-III Remastered loads
dxgi/d3d11 beside OpenGL). Judged by surface, every one of its frames looked
like the desktop and the blind never lifted: audio over a black panel for the
whole session. So the binding is stamped with the window's newest done commit
id the first time the override is seen, and the startup blind holds until a
done commit newer than that arrives, from whichever surface carries the game's
frames; commit ids come from one counter, so "newer" is an id comparison. A
DXVK guest keeps the startup blank until its first presented frame (its desktop
commit precedes the swapchain); a GL guest unblanks on its first XShmPutImage
frame.

The same override can also go away and come back. The WSI shim binds the window
at a swapchain's first commit and now tears that surface down when the guest
destroys it (drm #64), so a device loss or a rebuild on a new surface leaves a
gap with no binding, one that closes a frame or two later. Blanking on the
absence alone would put a black frame on the panel for a gap that is about to
close. So a gap only means "the desktop is all that is left" once the window has
painted again from something that is not the game -- which is what wine's
desktop does when the game's window goes away, and what a recreate does not do.

The blind's one-shot log line states the stamped id and the newest done commit
id at every transition, so the panel evidence can be tied to the comparison.

